          "source_structure_hash": "sha256:fd0ce06d637ed746aa87e880cdcc4ec1404f59393c83a26c45950265b88a64f5",
          "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nInformally we use the notion of a <TEXTITOPEN_0001>class<TEXTITCLOSE_0001>. Given a formula\n<MATH_0001>, we call\n<MATH_0002>\na <TEXTITOPEN_0002>class<TEXTITCLOSE_0002>. A class is easier to manipulate than the formula\nthat defines it, but it is not strictly speaking a mathematical\nobject. For example, if <MATH_0003> is a ring, then we may\nconsider the class of all <MATH_0004>-modules (since after all we\nmay translate the sentence ``<MATH_0005> is an <MATH_0006>-module''\ninto a formula in set theory, which then defines a class).\nA <TEXTITOPEN_0003>proper class<TEXTITCLOSE_0003> is a class which is not a set.\n\n<STRUCT_0002><STRUCT_0003>\nIn this way we may consider the category of <MATH_0007>-modules,\nwhich is a ``big'' category---in other words, it has a\nproper class of objects. Similarly, we may consider\nthe ``big'' category of schemes, the ``big'' category\nof rings, etc.\n\n\n\n",
          "source_text_hash": "sha256:cd62a3651a538f7ebbf2ec6d0fdd988db801b8bffbc68791ced45988b61e74dd",
          "unit_id": "tag:000A:prose-0001"
        }
